  "query_preview": "..\nSunday through Monday: In the wake of the cold front that passed \nthrough yesterday, the Inland Northwest will remain between \ntroughing off the PNW coast and a strong ridge over the central US \nfor the next couple days. This will enhance the thermal gradient \nacross the Cascades each afternoon and evening, bringing breezy to \ngusty conditions across central and eastern Washington. Areas that \nwill see the highest winds (25 to 30 mph) each day include the \nOkanogan Valley, Waterville Plateau, and Columbia Basin. When \ncoupled with minimum relative humidity values 15 to 25 percent each \nevening, elevated to critical fire weather conditions are expected \nin these areas. This will result in rapid fire spread for both new \nand existing fires. Temperatures today and Monday will cool to the \nmid to high 80s, right around normal for this time of year, \nresulting in only Minor HeatRisk. \n\nTuesday: On Tuesday, the low off the PNW coast will move inland, \nushering another dry cold front through the forecast area. This will \nbring widespread wind gusts 15 to 20 mph, and more localized higher \nwind gusts 25 to 35 mph in the usual areas: Cascade gaps, Waterville \nPlateau, Columbia Basin, and Okanogan Valley areas. With relative \nhumidity values remaining low at 15 to 25 percent, this will bring \ncritical fire weather conditions back to these areas. Temperatures \non Tuesday remain near normal. \n\nWednesday through Friday: Once the cold front moves through, the \nregion will again be in between offshore troughing and a ridge over \nthe central US. The thermal pressure gradient will be weaker than on \nMonday and Tuesday, bringing breezy winds 10 to 20 mph to central \nand eastern Washington. However, the ridge will begin moving \nwestward and strengthening during the last two days of the work \nweek, resulting in increasing temperatures and a return to the 90s \non Friday. This will also increase the thermal pressure gradient, \nbringing slightly higher wind gusts near 25 mph throughout the \nbasin, Waterville Plateau, and Okanogan Valley areas. \n\nNext weekend: Long-term guidance and clusters both favor another \ntrough moving across the region and ushering yet another dry cold \nfront through the area on Saturday. This will again increase winds \nacross the entire forecast area, and relative humidity values \nremaining low will result in another round of critical fire weather \nconditions. Throughout the week into next weekend, chances for \nprecipitation remain at or near zero percent, with PWAT values \nexpected to remain only at 70 percent of normal (0.5 to 0.7 inches). \n/AS",
